# Target B — cross-grain virtual determinant floor

**Date:** 2026-10-02  
**Base main:** `46cdb4fd3cb5f078f298fa51857e23e321ac9204`  
**Status:** proved candidate, noncanonical. Target B remains open.

Fix Section-50 parameters \(0<\eta,\lambda<1\). Delete crossings with angular
defect \(>D^{-1+\eta}\), then prune train vertices of current degree
\(<\lambda D\). Let \(H_1,\dots,H_k\) be the retained components. Write the
three phase-class sizes in \(H_i\) as \(M_i\ge b_i\ge a_i\).

Theorem 50.1 gives

\[
|E_{\rm hi}|\le C_\delta D^{2-\eta}=o(D^2)
\]

and minimum retained degree at least \(\lambda D\).

## Lemma 1 — every retained grain has two linear phase classes

For every retained component,

\[
\boxed{b_i\ge \lambda D/2.}
\]

A train in the largest phase class has no neighbor in its own class because every
retained crossing changes the \(\mathbb Z/3\) phase potential by \(\pm1\). Its
degree is at most \(a_i+b_i\le2b_i\), while retained degree is at least
\(\lambda D\).

## Lemma 2 — four-direction determinant floor

For every real \(\theta\),

\[
\boxed{
2|\sin\theta|+
|\sin(\theta+\pi/3)|+
|\sin(\theta-\pi/3)|
\ge\sqrt3.
}
\]

Indeed,

\[
|\sin(\theta+\pi/3)|+|\sin(\theta-\pi/3)|
\ge\sqrt3|\cos\theta|,
\]

and the result follows from
\(2|\sin\theta|+\sqrt3|\cos\theta|
\ge\sqrt3(|\sin\theta|+|\cos\theta|)\ge\sqrt3\).

## Uniform phase coherence

Put

\[
L_0=\left\lceil {3T\over\lambda D}\right\rceil,
\qquad
\varepsilon_D=L_0D^{-1+\eta}.
\]

Since \(T=O(D)\), \(\varepsilon_D=o(1)\). Theorem 50.1 and the signed lift
formula imply that every train in phase class \(r\) of \(H_i\) is within
\(\varepsilon_D\), projectively, of \(\alpha_i+r\pi/3\).

## Theorem — quadratic cross-grain virtual mass

For distinct retained components \(H_i,H_j\), define

\[
W_{ij}=
\sum_{\substack{\tau\in H_i,\ \sigma\in H_j\\
\tau,\sigma\ {\rm do\ not\ cross}}}
|\det(u_\tau,u_\sigma)|.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
W_{ij}\ge
(\sqrt3-8\varepsilon_D)b_ib_j
-|E_{\rm hi}(H_i,H_j)|.
}
\tag{1}
\]

Consequently,

\[
\boxed{
W_{ij}\ge{\sqrt3\over4}\lambda^2D^2-o(D^2).
}
\tag{2}
\]

Choose the two largest phase classes in each component. Their ideal direction sets
can be written

\[
\{\alpha,\alpha+\pi/3\},
\qquad
\{\beta,\beta+\pi/3\}.
\]

Each of the four class products has at least \(b_ib_j\) pairs. Actual angles differ
from ideal by at most \(2\varepsilon_D\). Since \(x\mapsto|\sin x|\) is
1-Lipschitz, Lemma 2 gives determinant mass at least
\(b_ib_j(\sqrt3-8\varepsilon_D)\) before physical crossing pairs are removed.

If a train in \(H_i\) physically crosses one in \(H_j\), that edge must be in the
initial high-defect deletion. Otherwise both retained endpoints would still be
joined and would lie in the same retained component. Removing physical crossing
pairs loses at most their number because \(|\det|\le1\). This proves (1), and
Lemma 1 gives (2).

## Global form

Let \(V_{\rm cross}=\sum_{i<j}W_{ij}\). High-defect edges are charged at most once,
so

\[
\boxed{
V_{\rm cross}
\ge
(\sqrt3-8\varepsilon_D)\sum_{i<j}b_ib_j-|E_{\rm hi}|
=
\sqrt3\sum_{i<j}b_ib_j-o(D^2).
}
\tag{3}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
{V_{\rm cross}\over D^2}
\ge
{\sqrt3\lambda^2\over4}\binom{k}{2}-o(1).
}
\tag{4}
\]

Thus every additional retained dense grain forces a positive quadratic amount of
cross-grain noncrossing determinant mass, regardless of relative grain phase.

The exact virtual-zonotope identity gives
\(V_{\rm miss}=\operatorname{area}(Z)-\operatorname{area}(\mathcal R)\), with
\(V_{\rm cross}\le V_{\rm miss}\). In a pure disk,
\(\operatorname{per}(Z)=2T\), hence
\(\operatorname{area}(Z)\le T^2/\pi\). Section 36 and
\(G+E<c_0B\) give
\(\operatorname{area}(\mathcal R)=\pi D^2/4-O(D)\). Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\sqrt3\sum_{i<j}b_ib_j
\le
{T^2\over\pi}-{\pi D^2\over4}+o(D^2).
}
\tag{5}
\]

If \(x=T/D\),

\[
\boxed{
{\sqrt3\lambda^2\over4}\binom{k}{2}
\le
{x^2\over\pi}-{\pi\over4}+o(1).
}
\tag{6}
\]

## Scope

This is independent of the current Target C-II fan and inactive-chamber work and
does not use GPI's dominant-component hypothesis.

It does not close Target B. Virtual determinant mass is not automatically Oler
surplus. The remaining step is to convert the cross-grain mass in (3), or the
complementary pruned-train virtual mass, into \(G+E\ge c_0B\) or a strict
smaller-diameter competitor.
